with Jessica's children

00:00:51.435 --> 00:00:52.843
and we are having a wonderful time,

00:00:52.843 --> 00:00:54.986
despite the subject might
have been quite intense.

00:00:54.986 --> 00:00:55.819
- That's what I was going to say!

00:00:55.819 --> 00:00:56.652
- We actually had a wonderful time making,

00:00:56.652 --> 00:00:57.653
and I made the error.

00:00:57.653 --> 00:00:59.265
I mean this is horrendous parenting

00:00:59.265 --> 00:01:01.392
but of showing them a little bit

00:01:01.392 --> 00:01:02.952
of the trailer and, and it transpires.

00:01:02.952 --> 00:01:05.640
They had thought that I
was playing The Good Nurse,

00:01:05.640 --> 00:01:07.400
and in this film I'm very,

00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:08.700
and they were so disappointed.

00:01:08.700 --> 00:01:09.533
They're like,

00:01:09.533 --> 00:01:10.366
- Very much not, so.

00:01:10.366 --> 00:01:11.199
- So, you're the bad nurse.

00:01:11.199 --> 00:01:12.032
And I was like, "Yeah."

00:01:12.032 --> 00:01:13.873
They definitely prefer me as a wizard.

00:01:13.873 --> 00:01:15.081
- Oh my God.

00:01:15.081 --> 00:01:15.974
Oh my God.

00:01:15.974 --> 00:01:18.557
I know you're one of those,
you're, you're, it's rare

00:01:18.557 --> 00:01:21.053
you're one of those actors
that you watch and you're like,

00:01:21.053 --> 00:01:23.910
"Are you a little bit of a serial killer?"

00:01:23.910 --> 00:01:27.027
Like, 'cause, 'cause you're so good at it.

00:01:27.027 --> 00:01:29.971
And then I'm like, "Is he that good?"

00:01:29.971 --> 00:01:32.051
I was like, "It was incredible."

00:01:32.051 --> 00:01:33.331
My sister and I were watching last night,

00:01:33.331 --> 00:01:34.312
and I was just like, "Oh my God!"

00:01:34.312 --> 00:01:35.272
She's visiting from out of town

00:01:35.272 --> 00:01:37.074
and she was like, "This is crazy good."

00:01:37.074 --> 00:01:39.229
And anyway, so what
are your kids into now?

00:01:39.229 --> 00:01:40.636
They like Fantastic Beasts, but what else?

00:01:40.636 --> 00:01:41.947
- Well, they, they,

00:01:41.947 --> 00:01:44.753
I mean I have a boy who
loves a bit of Paw Patrol,

00:01:44.753 --> 00:01:46.075
- [Kelly] Oh, I love Paw Patrol, yeah.

00:01:46.075 --> 00:01:47.408
- Love a bit of Octonauts, loves,

00:01:47.408 --> 00:01:49.809
I mean if the truth be told, he just like,

00:01:49.809 --> 00:01:52.151
likes to make anything into a weapon.

00:01:52.151 --> 00:01:52.984
- Yeah.

00:01:52.984 --> 00:01:53.817
- Which is probably horrendous.

00:01:53.817 --> 00:01:54.650
- Yeah.

00:01:54.650 --> 00:01:55.483
- And I don't know,

00:01:55.483 --> 00:01:56.953
I promise I'm quite,
I'm not a serial killer.

00:01:56.953 --> 00:01:58.268
I'm a quite gentle person.

00:01:58.268 --> 00:02:00.721
But like anything like he'll
turn that into a weapon.

00:02:00.721 --> 00:02:01.554
- Yeah.

00:02:01.554 --> 00:02:02.470
- Even some flowers or something.

00:02:02.470 --> 00:02:03.985
Come in the morning with flowers.

00:02:03.985 --> 00:02:04.818
- Yeah.

00:02:04.818 --> 00:02:05.651
- I don't know what's going wrong.

00:02:05.651 --> 00:02:06.484
How about your little ones?

00:02:06.484 --> 00:02:07.317
- Like, my boy's like that.

00:02:07.317 --> 00:02:08.150
Yeah.

00:02:08.150 --> 00:02:08.983
My girl is kind of like that too.

00:02:08.983 --> 00:02:09.816
- Really?

00:02:09.816 --> 00:02:10.649
- Yeah.

00:02:10.649 --> 00:02:11.482
It's just a crazy house.

00:02:11.482 --> 00:02:12.315
Yeah.

00:02:12.315 --> 00:02:13.361
They, they, they both like Aquaman.

00:02:13.361 --> 00:02:14.650
So they both got tridents

00:02:14.650 --> 00:02:16.497
and I was like, "This was a bad idea."

00:02:16.497 --> 00:02:17.330
- Yeah.

00:02:17.330 --> 00:02:18.163
- Yeah.

00:02:18.163 --> 00:02:19.035
'Cause things have been broken-

00:02:19.035 --> 00:02:19.932
- And you go to the park-

00:02:19.932 --> 00:02:20.901
- and they nail each other with them.

00:02:20.901 --> 00:02:22.054
- The everything is a sword and-

00:02:22.054 --> 00:02:22.887
- Yep.

00:02:22.887 --> 00:02:23.720
- And just, we, we just

00:02:23.720 --> 00:02:25.073
collected a load of
twigs now that are just

00:02:25.073 --> 00:02:25.906
like sticks that-

00:02:25.906 --> 00:02:26.739
- He collects sticks?

00:02:26.739 --> 00:02:27.572
My son does that too.

00:02:27.572 --> 00:02:28.405
- I know.

00:02:28.405 --> 00:02:29.238
- Everywhere we go,

00:02:29.238 --> 00:02:30.245
I'm like, "No, we cannot
take this one home.

00:02:30.245 --> 00:02:31.078
You have like ten!"

00:02:31.078 --> 00:02:33.478
- Like a trunk of the
car filled with sticks.

00:02:33.478 --> 00:02:36.806
But much better than, than
buying plastic toys, I suppose.

00:02:36.806 --> 00:02:37.916
- I guess. I guess.

00:02:37.916 --> 00:02:39.793
Yeah, I know, I guess we're the jerks

00:02:39.793 --> 00:02:40.698
in that situation.

00:02:40.698 --> 00:02:42.479
I hear, does one of your,

00:02:42.479 --> 00:02:43.590
is one of your kids musical?

00:02:43.590 --> 00:02:44.423
Did I hear?

00:02:44.423 --> 00:02:46.502
- Oh well my daughter Iris. My daughter.

00:02:46.502 --> 00:02:47.366
So, Broken Voice.

00:02:47.366 --> 00:02:49.382
- Yeah, I love that it's
Iris, that's so beautiful.

00:02:49.382 --> 00:02:51.418
Oh, that she's just
started playing the piano.

00:02:51.418 --> 00:02:52.251
Yeah.

00:02:52.251 --> 00:02:53.084
- Oh, that's great.

00:02:53.084 --> 00:02:53.917
- And they both love singing,

00:02:53.917 --> 00:02:55.951
so they, yeah, it's...

00:02:55.951 --> 00:02:56.784
And I love it.

00:02:56.784 --> 00:02:59.301
I love, it's just been
these first few days

00:02:59.301 --> 00:03:00.848
and she's really enjoying it.

00:03:00.848 --> 00:03:03.280
Piano practice is still
a lovely novel thing.

00:03:03.280 --> 00:03:04.113
- Yeah.

00:03:04.113 --> 00:03:05.520
- Whereas, when I was younger,

00:03:05.520 --> 00:03:07.291
I mean when I started playing the piano,

00:03:07.291 --> 00:03:10.512
I used to, the days I had
to go to piano lessons,

00:03:10.512 --> 00:03:14.452
I would write a note saying
that I'd run away from home.

00:03:14.452 --> 00:03:15.600
- Yeah, you like, dreaded it.

00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:18.118
- My mom would come in like,
"Ed, it's piano practice."

00:03:18.118 --> 00:03:21.015
I'm like, "Did you not get the
notes saying I'd run away?"

00:03:21.015 --> 00:03:22.534
She's like, "Yeah, but
you do that every week."

00:03:22.534 --> 00:03:23.867
So.

00:03:23.867 --> 00:03:24.900
- So do you still play though?

00:03:24.900 --> 00:03:25.733
- I do.

00:03:25.733 --> 00:03:26.864
That's a beautiful,
that's a beautiful thing.

00:03:26.864 --> 00:03:28.593
- Yeah, yeah. Do you play?

00:03:28.593 --> 00:03:30.480
- I play poorly.

00:03:30.480 --> 00:03:31.313
- [Eddie] Well, so do I.

00:03:31.313 --> 00:03:32.146
- Yes.

00:03:32.146 --> 00:03:32.979
No, no, no, no.

00:03:32.979 --> 00:03:33.812
- Poorly but passionately, Kelly.

00:03:33.812 --> 00:03:34.645
- [Kelly] No.

00:03:34.645 --> 00:03:35.478
- No?

00:03:35.478 --> 00:03:36.311
- No, no.

00:03:36.311 --> 00:03:37.819
I am so passionate
about writing in general

00:03:37.819 --> 00:03:40.816
and like I love, I think
my problem is I met Jason,

00:03:40.816 --> 00:03:41.649
my musical director,

00:03:41.649 --> 00:03:42.482
like right after Idol

00:03:42.482 --> 00:03:43.315
and like I,

00:03:43.315 --> 00:03:44.398
like 20 years ago.

00:03:44.398 --> 00:03:46.925
And once you, once you sing with someone

00:03:46.925 --> 00:03:48.078
that plays like that.

00:03:48.078 --> 00:03:48.911
- Yeah.

00:03:48.911 --> 00:03:49.744
- It's daunting.

00:03:49.744 --> 00:03:51.108
You're like, "I'm never gonna be able to."

00:03:51.108 --> 00:03:52.953
I even tried one tour and
he like worked with me

00:03:52.953 --> 00:03:54.244
and I was like "No."

00:03:54.244 --> 00:03:55.077
- [Eddie] No.

00:03:55.077 --> 00:03:55.993
- It's never gonna be
like how it sounds to you.

00:03:55.993 --> 00:03:57.572
And that's not fair to
the people listening."

00:03:57.572 --> 00:03:58.405
So, but-

00:03:58.405 --> 00:04:00.366
- Also the nerves that
you get playing the piano.

00:04:00.366 --> 00:04:01.623
Like I find singing okay. In front people.

00:04:01.623 --> 00:04:02.456
- [Kelly] Me too.

00:04:02.456 --> 00:04:03.289
- But like the feeling of your,

00:04:03.289 --> 00:04:04.122
the wobble of-

00:04:04.122 --> 00:04:04.955
- Yes!

00:04:04.955 --> 00:04:05.788
- Your hands before playing live.

00:04:05.788 --> 00:04:06.724
- And like all of a sudden I'm like,

00:04:06.724 --> 00:04:07.557
I don't know any of the notes.

00:04:07.557 --> 00:04:08.398
I don't know what I just did.

00:04:08.398 --> 00:04:09.593
I don't, I don't remember what I just did.

00:04:09.593 --> 00:04:10.754
I just played the whole song.

00:04:10.754 --> 00:04:11.598
Yeah. I get very nervous.

00:04:11.598 --> 00:04:13.390
- It's so thrilling to hear
that you have the same thing.

00:04:13.390 --> 00:04:14.668
- Yeah, but I don't get that with singing.

00:04:14.668 --> 00:04:15.501
- It's odd.

00:04:15.501 --> 00:04:16.334
- Yeah.

00:04:16.334 --> 00:04:17.167
- Really odd.

00:04:17.167 --> 00:04:19.192
- So we lost Queen Elizabeth this year,

00:04:19.192 --> 00:04:20.322
and not long ago,

00:04:20.322 --> 00:04:21.293
I wanna get this right.

00:04:21.293 --> 00:04:22.126
She made you an

00:04:22.126 --> 00:04:26.959
Officer Of The Most Excellent
Order Of The British Empire.

00:04:28.475 --> 00:04:29.974
- That's a lot of words.

00:04:29.974 --> 00:04:32.376
- I feel like it's very fancy,

00:04:32.376 --> 00:04:34.136
and I just wanna know what it means.

00:04:34.136 --> 00:04:35.351
- Very good question.

00:04:35.351 --> 00:04:38.427
I'm not sure I can entirely
tell you what it means

00:04:38.427 --> 00:04:41.368
but it was, it was an
extraordinary moment.

00:04:41.368 --> 00:04:43.064
You get, you get a medal.

00:04:43.064 --> 00:04:43.897
- Yeah.

00:04:43.897 --> 00:04:45.997
- And you line up in Windsor Castle,

00:04:45.997 --> 00:04:50.882
alongside these people who've
done really brilliant things.

00:04:50.882 --> 00:04:53.856
Like, you know, sort of people in charge

00:04:53.856 --> 00:04:54.893
of the health service

00:04:54.893 --> 00:04:56.439
or who've sort of running the

00:04:56.439 --> 00:04:59.062
you know, who've sort of curing cancer.

00:04:59.062 --> 00:04:59.895
- [Kelly] That kind of thing.

00:04:59.895 --> 00:05:01.005
- And you are the person who

00:05:01.005 --> 00:05:02.338
like, you know, played the part.

00:05:02.338 --> 00:05:03.522
So you feel sort of pretty out of,

00:05:03.522 --> 00:05:05.453
but also you get a lesson

00:05:05.453 --> 00:05:06.552
in how to genuflect,

00:05:06.552 --> 00:05:08.806
in how to kind of what to say

00:05:08.806 --> 00:05:09.666
and when to move forward

00:05:09.666 --> 00:05:11.949
and when we were being told this,

00:05:11.949 --> 00:05:13.741
it just for me, went in one ear

00:05:13.741 --> 00:05:17.397
and out the other and,
and I went in to meet

00:05:17.397 --> 00:05:18.573
the Queen,

00:05:18.573 --> 00:05:20.162
and, everything went out.

00:05:20.162 --> 00:05:23.000
And I just sort of mumbled
and kept doing that basically.

00:05:23.000 --> 00:05:25.794
But it was a very, very special moment.

00:05:25.794 --> 00:05:27.736
- I just love that you
can say that sentence.

00:05:27.736 --> 00:05:29.453
"When I walked in to meet the Queen."

00:05:29.453 --> 00:05:30.286
- Queen, Oh.

00:05:30.286 --> 00:05:31.512
- Like, it's just like
a really cool sentence

00:05:31.512 --> 00:05:32.345
to be able to say.

00:05:32.345 --> 00:05:33.660
- But we have these odd things in it.

00:05:33.660 --> 00:05:35.252
There was another sort

00:05:35.252 --> 00:05:36.545
of thing that I got which was called

00:05:36.545 --> 00:05:38.682
the Freedom of the City of London.

00:05:38.682 --> 00:05:40.938
And it's a very beautiful sort

00:05:40.938 --> 00:05:42.763
of certificate you get
and you go to a meet.

00:05:42.763 --> 00:05:43.596
I was offered it,

00:05:43.596 --> 00:05:44.429
and I was like, "God, how wonderful."

00:05:44.429 --> 00:05:45.262
It's very historical.

00:05:45.262 --> 00:05:47.220
I can't tell you exactly what it is.

00:05:47.220 --> 00:05:48.917
But when I went to the ceremony,

00:05:48.917 --> 00:05:51.093
they said, "And what you get,

00:05:51.093 --> 00:05:52.551
for this,

00:05:52.551 --> 00:05:54.782
is you are now legally allowed

00:05:54.782 --> 00:05:57.782
to lead sheep across London Bridge."

00:05:59.999 --> 00:06:03.327
And I get to lead sheep
across London Bridge.

00:06:03.327 --> 00:06:04.949
And, and-

00:06:04.949 --> 00:06:07.039
- This is a really old award.

00:06:07.039 --> 00:06:08.394
This has to be a very old award.

00:06:08.394 --> 00:06:09.535
- [Eddie] Very old award.

00:06:09.535 --> 00:06:10.368
- I mean, I've-

00:06:10.368 --> 00:06:11.231
- [Kelly] Do you own sheep?

00:06:11.231 --> 00:06:13.610
- I don't own sheep.

00:06:13.610 --> 00:06:15.445
- You should get some
just to walk it across

00:06:15.445 --> 00:06:17.161
and be like, "I can do this. You can't."

00:06:17.161 --> 00:06:18.922
- Can you imagine?

00:06:18.922 --> 00:06:19.755
- [Kelly] Yes!

00:06:19.755 --> 00:06:21.192
- I mean I have felt like
I want to put that to test.

00:06:21.192 --> 00:06:23.985
I think if I really sort
of took some sheep on leads

00:06:23.985 --> 00:06:24.923
across London Bridge-

00:06:24.923 --> 00:06:25.756
- [Kelly] I really want to see it.

00:06:25.756 --> 00:06:26.726
- I might get stopped by the police.

00:06:26.726 --> 00:06:30.415
- Who cares? It's, show your certificate!

00:06:30.415 --> 00:06:31.613
- Brandishing my certificate.

00:06:31.613 --> 00:06:32.446
- Yeah.

00:06:32.446 --> 00:06:33.279
- Just hold it.

00:06:33.279 --> 00:06:34.112
Have it like hanging,

00:06:34.112 --> 00:06:35.944
like the spelling bee people.

00:06:35.944 --> 00:06:36.777
- Redmayne's lost it.

00:06:36.777 --> 00:06:37.610
- [Kelly] Oh, my god!

00:06:37.610 --> 00:06:38.526
- He's like lost the plot.

00:06:38.526 --> 00:06:40.276
I just think that's amazing
'cause it's such an old thing.

00:06:40.276 --> 00:06:41.896
That probably was a huge deal back then,

00:06:41.896 --> 00:06:42.780
that you could actually-

00:06:42.780 --> 00:06:43.613
- Take the sheep across.

00:06:43.613 --> 00:06:44.446
- Take the sheep.

00:06:44.446 --> 00:06:46.126
Well, because that is, for farmers.

00:06:46.126 --> 00:06:46.959
That's good.

00:06:46.959 --> 00:06:47.877
You get like different
grass over there, whatever.

00:06:47.877 --> 00:06:49.448
That's probably a really great thing's.

00:06:49.448 --> 00:06:50.333
- Massive deal. Yeah.

00:06:50.333 --> 00:06:51.240
- Not so much now.

00:06:51.240 --> 00:06:52.073
- Well, I mean.

00:06:52.073 --> 00:06:53.392
- Until you try it.

00:06:53.392 --> 00:06:54.225
- Until I try it.

00:06:54.225 --> 00:06:55.058
- Yeah.

00:06:55.058 --> 00:06:56.402
Let's talk about The Good Nurse.

00:06:56.402 --> 00:06:59.666
So what I always wonder
when I see these characters

00:06:59.666 --> 00:07:01.778
'cause what draws you to do that?

00:07:01.778 --> 00:07:04.093
Because it's an insane character.

00:07:04.093 --> 00:07:08.139
- Mm. Do you know, I knew
nothing about this story.

00:07:08.139 --> 00:07:08.972
- [Kelly] Me neither.

00:07:08.972 --> 00:07:09.805
And when I read the script,

00:07:09.805 --> 00:07:10.638
the character that

00:07:10.638 --> 00:07:11.869
Jessica Chastain plays is this woman,

00:07:11.869 --> 00:07:14.017
Amy Loughren is like a,

00:07:14.017 --> 00:07:15.451
she's a superhero, really.

00:07:15.451 --> 00:07:16.284
- Yeah.

00:07:16.284 --> 00:07:17.117
- What she managed to-

00:07:17.117 --> 00:07:17.950
- Like a real life one. Yeah.

00:07:17.950 --> 00:07:19.033
- A real life superhero.

00:07:19.033 --> 00:07:23.280
And so I found it
extraordinarily compelling

00:07:23.280 --> 00:07:25.456
and this guy Charlie Cullen,

00:07:25.456 --> 00:07:26.299
who I played,

00:07:26.299 --> 00:07:29.424
who was very much a bad nurse,

00:07:29.424 --> 00:07:31.248
he was also kind of riveting

00:07:31.248 --> 00:07:33.349
because I got to speak
to the real Amy Loughren.

00:07:33.349 --> 00:07:35.579
And she said there were
just two people in her life.

00:07:35.579 --> 00:07:38.300
On the one hand he was
this very gentle, kind,

00:07:38.300 --> 00:07:41.124
self deprecating, quite funny man.

00:07:41.124 --> 00:07:42.917
And then she met twice this,

00:07:42.917 --> 00:07:44.869
this different human being.

00:07:44.869 --> 00:07:48.133
And, and so the story was
not only of this hero,

00:07:48.133 --> 00:07:50.544
but also how like the systems had failed.

00:07:50.544 --> 00:07:53.584
And I found it unlike
anything I'd read. So.

00:07:53.584 --> 00:07:54.510
- Did you have to study?

00:07:54.510 --> 00:07:55.931
Like to get the terminology like medical?

00:07:55.931 --> 00:07:57.915
- Yeah, so, so Jessica Chastain and I

00:07:57.915 --> 00:07:59.952
were sent to nurse school.

00:07:59.952 --> 00:08:00.804
- Oh, that's cool.

00:08:00.804 --> 00:08:03.664
- Which was actually, really intriguing.

00:08:03.664 --> 00:08:05.179
But it proved one thing,

00:08:05.179 --> 00:08:08.883
which is I'm a like
completely useless nurse.

00:08:08.883 --> 00:08:11.227
Never turn to me in a crisis.

00:08:11.227 --> 00:08:13.897
It will, it will end in tears.

00:08:13.897 --> 00:08:14.730
Jess was very,

00:08:14.730 --> 00:08:16.709
Jess was brilliant cause
she was very decisive.

00:08:16.709 --> 00:08:17.808
She's a decisive nurse.

00:08:17.808 --> 00:08:19.969
She like knows exactly.

00:08:19.969 --> 00:08:21.283
You know, she just goes for it.

00:08:21.283 --> 00:08:22.875
Whereas, I was quite dithering.

00:08:22.875 --> 00:08:24.283
No one wants a dithering nurse.

00:08:24.283 --> 00:08:25.116
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:08:25.116 --> 00:08:25.949
- But...

00:08:25.949 --> 00:08:27.611
- I don't even know anyone
that uses the word dithering.

00:08:27.611 --> 00:08:30.384
So you know what? You, congratulations.

00:08:30.384 --> 00:08:31.217
- Thank you.

00:08:31.217 --> 00:08:32.240
- Like word of the day.

00:08:32.240 --> 00:08:33.390
- So I, yeah, but they,

00:08:33.390 --> 00:08:34.437
but they would be all these sort

00:08:34.437 --> 00:08:36.336
of poor dummies that had been brought in,

00:08:36.336 --> 00:08:38.543
and the director would find me on set

00:08:38.543 --> 00:08:39.771
in the morning with all these sort

00:08:39.771 --> 00:08:42.020
of needles trying to trying to work out

00:08:42.020 --> 00:08:43.128
if I could make the thing.

00:08:43.128 --> 00:08:43.961
It was, yeah.

00:08:43.961 --> 00:08:44.794
It was all a bit of a nightmare.

00:08:44.794 --> 00:08:46.544
And there was a real nurse on set.

00:08:46.544 --> 00:08:47.920
A lovely man called Joe.

00:08:47.920 --> 00:08:50.309
And after each take, not
only would you be looking

00:08:50.309 --> 00:08:52.133
for, you know, to see with the director

00:08:52.133 --> 00:08:54.369
whether you'd done it okay.

00:08:54.369 --> 00:08:55.435
- You'd look down to see-

00:08:55.435 --> 00:08:57.456
- More terrifyingly, it was
like, "Joe did I? Did it?"

00:08:57.456 --> 00:08:58.289
And you know

00:08:58.289 --> 00:09:00.539
But he was brilliant and
specific, which is cool.

00:09:00.539 --> 00:09:01.829
- Yeah. And honest?

00:09:01.829 --> 00:09:03.663
- Very honest. Not missing his voice.

00:09:03.663 --> 00:09:05.616
- I gathered that from your tone.

00:09:05.616 --> 00:09:08.443
- Y'all have got to see
this movie, it's so good.

00:09:08.443 --> 00:09:10.405
The Good Nurse is in theaters now!

00:09:10.405 --> 00:09:12.089
And on Netflix October 26th!

